E=[]
# --- part 1: turning points of the modern world
E+=[
X('magna-carta-1215','Magna Carta (1215): a failed peace treaty that became a legend of liberty',H,'confirmed',1,1215,['documentary','textual'],
 "On 15 June 1215, at Runnymede beside the River Thames, King John of England agreed to a charter drawn up to make peace with rebel barons. Its most quoted clause promised that no free man would be imprisoned or stripped of his rights except by the lawful judgement of his peers or by the law of the land. As a peace deal it failed within weeks: Pope Innocent III annulled it in August 1215 and civil war resumed. It was reissued in revised form in 1216, 1217 and 1225, and the 1217 reissue was accompanied by a separate Charter of the Forest. Four original copies of the 1215 charter survive.",
 [('A myth built later','In the 17th century the lawyer Sir Edward Coke presented Magna Carta as a guarantee of ancient English liberties in his struggles with the Stuart kings. Historians note that in 1215 it mainly protected the barons and the Church, and that much of its later meaning was given to it by later generations, including the founders of the United States.'),
  ('The forgotten companion','The Charter of the Forest of 1217 restored common people\'s rights to use royal forests for grazing, firewood and food. It stayed in force in England longer than any other statute, until 1971.')],
 ['wiki:Magna_Carta','wiki:Charter_of_the_Forest','wiki:King_John,_King_of_England'],img='Magna Carta',
 changed='1215: sealed and annulled. 1216-1225: reissued. 1600s: Coke recasts it as ancient liberty. 1791: echoes in the US Bill of Rights. 2015: 800th anniversary.'),
X('ninety-five-theses-1517','Luther\'s Ninety-five Theses (1517): did the hammer ever strike the door?',H,'confirmed',1,1517,['documentary','textual'],
 "On 31 October 1517 Martin Luther, a monk and professor at Wittenberg in Saxony, sent a list of 95 propositions to Archbishop Albert of Mainz, criticising the sale of indulgences, certificates said to reduce punishment for sins after death. The preacher Johann Tetzel was selling them to raise money, partly to rebuild St Peter's Basilica in Rome. Printers soon spread the theses across Germany, helped by the new printing press. Luther refused to recant at the Diet of Worms in 1521 and was excommunicated. The movement he started split Western Christianity into Catholic and Protestant churches and led to more than a century of religious wars.",
 [('The door that may not have happened','The famous image of Luther nailing the theses to the door of Wittenberg\'s Castle Church comes from an account by his colleague Philip Melanchthon written after Luther\'s death. In 1961 the Catholic historian Erwin Iserloh argued that the nailing never took place; others point to a note by Luther\'s secretary Georg Rörer that supports it. The question is still debated.'),
  ('Printing as the real hammer','Historians often argue that the Reformation succeeded where earlier reformers such as Jan Hus failed partly because pamphlets could now be printed in thousands of copies within weeks.')],
 ['wiki:Ninety-five_Theses','wiki:Martin_Luther','wiki:Reformation'],img='Ninety-five Theses',
 changed='1517: theses sent. 1521: Diet of Worms. 1546: Melanchthon describes the nailing. 1961: Iserloh disputes it. 2017: 500th anniversary.'),
X('magellan-elcano-1519','Magellan and Elcano: the first voyage around the world, and the day that went missing',H,'confirmed',1,1519,['documentary','textual'],
 "In September 1519 five ships with about 270 men left Spain under the Portuguese navigator Ferdinand Magellan, seeking a western route to the Spice Islands. They found the strait at the tip of South America now named after him and crossed the Pacific, an ocean far wider than anyone in Europe expected; many sailors died of scurvy and hunger. Magellan was killed at Mactan in the Philippines in April 1521. Juan Sebastián Elcano brought the last ship, the Victoria, back to Spain in September 1522 with 18 of the original crew, completing the first known circumnavigation of the Earth.",
 [('The lost day','When the survivors reached the Cape Verde Islands in July 1522, they found that their carefully kept ship\'s calendar was one day behind the local date. Antonio Pigafetta, the expedition\'s chronicler, recorded the surprise. By sailing west around the world they had experienced one fewer sunrise than people at home, the problem later handled by the International Date Line.'),
  ('Who went round first?','Magellan\'s enslaved interpreter, known as Enrique of Malacca, had been taken from Southeast Asia to Europe years before. If he made his way home after Magellan\'s death, he may have been the first person to travel all the way around the world. His fate is unknown, so this remains speculative.')],
 ['wiki:Magellan_expedition','wiki:Juan_Sebastián_Elcano','wiki:Antonio_Pigafetta','wiki:Enrique_of_Malacca'],img='Magellan expedition',end=1522),
X('french-revolution-1789','The French Revolution (1789): bread, rumours and a new calendar',H,'confirmed',1,1789,['documentary','textual'],
 "In 1789 a financial crisis forced King Louis XVI of France to call the Estates-General. Its commoner deputies declared themselves a National Assembly, and on 14 July a Paris crowd stormed the Bastille fortress, which held only seven prisoners that day. The Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen followed in August. The monarchy was abolished in 1792 and the king was executed in January 1793. During the Terror of 1793 to 1794 thousands were guillotined. The revolutionaries introduced the metric system and a new calendar with ten-day weeks, counting years from the founding of the Republic. Napoleon Bonaparte seized power in 1799.",
 [('The Great Fear','In the summer of 1789 rumours spread through the French countryside that bands of brigands paid by aristocrats were coming to destroy the harvest. No such bands existed, but peasants armed themselves and attacked manor houses. The historian Georges Lefebvre studied this "Great Fear" in 1932 as a classic case of a rumour panic.'),
  ('"Let them eat cake"','The line is popularly put in the mouth of Queen Marie Antoinette. Historians find no evidence she said it; a similar phrase appears in Jean-Jacques Rousseau\'s Confessions, attributed to "a great princess", written when Marie Antoinette was still a child in Austria.'),
  ('Rival readings','Historians long read the Revolution as a class struggle of the rising middle class; from the 1960s, revisionist historians such as François Furet stressed political ideas and culture instead.')],
 ['wiki:French_Revolution','wiki:Great_Fear','wiki:French_Republican_calendar','wiki:Let_them_eat_cake'],img='Storming of the Bastille',end=1799),
X('watt-steam-engine','Steam power and the Industrial Revolution, and the ancient engine that came first',H,'confirmed',2,1769,['documentary','technological'],
 "Steam engines were first used to pump water out of mines: Thomas Newcomen's engine began working in 1712. In 1769 the Scottish instrument maker James Watt patented a separate condenser that made steam engines far more efficient, and from 1775 his partnership with the industrialist Matthew Boulton sold them to mills and factories. Steam power, coal, iron and new textile machines drove the Industrial Revolution, which began in Britain in the later 18th century and spread worldwide. It changed how most people lived and worked, and began the large rise in carbon dioxide emissions from burning fossil fuels.",
 [('An engine 1,700 years early','In the 1st century CE Hero of Alexandria described the aeolipile, a sphere spun by jets of steam. Historians debate why it stayed a curiosity: suggestions include cheap slave labour, lack of suitable metalworking, and no pressing need such as flooded coal mines.'),
  ('The kettle legend','A popular story says young Watt got the idea from watching the lid of a boiling kettle. It was told after his fame and is usually treated as a legend.')],
 ['wiki:Watt_steam_engine','wiki:Industrial_Revolution','wiki:Newcomen_atmospheric_engine','wiki:Aeolipile'],img='Watt steam engine',
 changed='1st c. CE: aeolipile. 1712: Newcomen engine. 1769: Watt condenser. 1800s: railways, steamships. 20th c.: link to climate change recognised.'),
X('meiji-restoration-1868','The Meiji Restoration (1868): the samurai age ends, and a rebel becomes a star',H,'confirmed',2,1868,['documentary','textual'],
 "In 1853 US warships under Commodore Matthew Perry arrived in Japan and forced the Tokugawa shogunate to open the country to trade. In 1868 a coalition of domains overthrew the shogun and restored power to the young Emperor Meiji. The new government abolished the feudal domains and the samurai as a class, introduced conscription, built railways and factories, and sent students abroad to learn Western science. In 1877 the Satsuma Rebellion, led by the samurai leader Saigō Takamori, was crushed and Saigō died. Within a few decades Japan became an industrial power.",
 [('Saigō in the sky','Legends about Saigō spread even before his death. Some said he had not died but had escaped to China or India; others that he had risen to the heavens as the planet Mars or a comet. In 1877 Mars was unusually close and bright, and woodblock prints showed him in heroic poses.'),
  ('The rebel pardoned','The government that crushed him pardoned him after his death, and his statue stands in Tokyo\'s Ueno Park. Stories that a fallen hero survived and may return are told of many figures, from King Arthur to Frederick Barbarossa.')],
 ['wiki:Meiji_Restoration','wiki:Saigō_Takamori','wiki:Satsuma_Rebellion'],img='Meiji Restoration',end=1889),
X('einstein-1905','Einstein\'s miracle year (1905): a patent clerk rewrites physics',H,'confirmed',1,1905,['textual','experimental'],
 "In 1905 Albert Einstein, then 26 and working as a technical expert at the Swiss patent office in Bern, published four papers. One explained the photoelectric effect by proposing that light comes in packets (quanta), a foundation of quantum theory. One explained Brownian motion, giving strong evidence that atoms exist. One set out special relativity, in which time and space depend on the observer's motion. A short follow-up showed that mass and energy are equivalent, E = mc². His 1921 Nobel Prize was awarded for the photoelectric effect, not for relativity.",
 [('Who got there first?','Hendrik Lorentz and Henri Poincaré had developed much of the mathematics of relativity before 1905. Historians generally credit Einstein with the new physical interpretation, though the question of priority has been debated since.'),
  ('Mileva Marić','Einstein\'s first wife, the physicist Mileva Marić, studied with him. Some writers have argued she co-authored the 1905 work; most historians of science say the surviving evidence does not show this, while acknowledging her role is not fully known.')],
 ['wiki:Annus_mirabilis_papers','wiki:Albert_Einstein','wiki:Special_relativity','wiki:Mileva_Marić'],img='Annus mirabilis papers',
 changed='1905: four papers. 1915: general relativity. 1919: eclipse test. 1921: Nobel Prize for the photoelectric effect.'),
X('salt-march-1930','The Salt March (1930): a pinch of salt against an empire',H,'confirmed',2,1930,['documentary','photographic'],
 "Under British rule in India, making or selling salt without paying tax was illegal. On 12 March 1930 Mohandas Gandhi set out from his ashram near Ahmedabad with 78 followers and walked about 390 km to the coastal village of Dandi, arriving on 6 April. There he picked up a lump of natural salt, breaking the law. Mass civil disobedience followed across India and tens of thousands of people were arrested. The march drew worldwide attention to the independence movement, which succeeded in 1947.",
 [('Dharasana','In May 1930 nonviolent volunteers marched on the Dharasana salt works and were beaten by police with steel-tipped clubs. The American journalist Webb Miller\'s report was carried around the world and shaped opinion of British rule.'),
  ('An idea that travelled','Martin Luther King Jr. and other leaders of the US civil rights movement studied Gandhi\'s methods. Critics, including some Indian revolutionaries of the time, argued that nonviolence alone could not have ended British rule.')],
 ['wiki:Salt_March','wiki:Mahatma_Gandhi','wiki:Dharasana_Satyagraha'],img='Salt March'),
X('partition-of-india-1947','The Partition of India (1947): a border drawn in five weeks',H,'confirmed',1,1947,['documentary','oral history'],
 "When British rule ended in August 1947, British India was divided into two independent states, India and Pakistan (including what is now Bangladesh). The British lawyer Cyril Radcliffe, who had never been to India before, was given about five weeks to draw the borders through Punjab and Bengal. The boundaries were published on 17 August, two days after independence. Between 10 and 20 million people moved across the new borders, and estimates of those killed in communal violence range from several hundred thousand to about two million.",
 [('Who is to blame?','Historians debate the share of responsibility of the British government, which brought the date of withdrawal forward from June 1948 to August 1947, and of the leaders of the Indian National Congress and the Muslim League.'),
  ('Recovering memory','For decades much of the experience was not recorded. Since the 2000s oral history projects such as the 1947 Partition Archive have collected thousands of testimonies from survivors.')],
 ['wiki:Partition_of_India','wiki:Radcliffe_Line','wiki:1947_Partition_Archive'],img='Partition of India'),
X('berlin-wall-1989','The fall of the Berlin Wall (1989): history changed by a misread note',H,'confirmed',1,1989,['documentary','film'],
 "East Germany built the Berlin Wall in August 1961 to stop its citizens fleeing to the West; at least 140 people died at it. On the evening of 9 November 1989, amid mass protests, government spokesman Günter Schabowski announced new travel rules at a televised press conference. He had not been properly briefed. Asked when they took effect, he looked at his note and said, 'immediately, without delay'. Thousands of East Berliners went to the border crossings. At Bornholmer Straße the officer in charge, Harald Jäger, opened the gate that night. Germany was reunified on 3 October 1990, and the Soviet Union dissolved in December 1991.",
 [('An accident or a push?','Historians stress that the press-conference mistake only triggered a collapse already under way, driven by protests, refugees fleeing through Hungary and Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev\'s refusal to use force.')],
 ['wiki:Fall_of_the_Berlin_Wall','wiki:Günter_Schabowski','wiki:Berlin_Wall'],img='Fall of the Berlin Wall'),
X('end-of-apartheid-1994','The end of apartheid (1990-1994), and the "Mandela effect"',H,'confirmed',1,1990,['documentary','film','oral history'],
 "Nelson Mandela was released on 11 February 1990 after 27 years in prison, and the ban on the African National Congress and other movements was lifted. Negotiations between the government of F. W. de Klerk, the ANC and other parties followed, amid violence that killed thousands; the assassination of Chris Hani in April 1993 brought the country close to civil war. South Africa's first elections open to all races were held on 27 April 1994, now Freedom Day, and Mandela was inaugurated as president on 10 May 1994. A Truth and Reconciliation Commission heard testimony about crimes of the apartheid era.",
 [('The Mandela effect','In about 2009 the writer Fiona Broome found that many people remembered Mandela dying in prison in the 1980s, although he died in 2013. She named this the "Mandela effect", and some online writers took it as evidence of parallel realities. Psychologists explain such shared mistakes as false memories, which people can easily form and share.')],
 ['wiki:Negotiations_to_end_apartheid_in_South_Africa','wiki:Nelson_Mandela','wiki:Truth_and_Reconciliation_Commission_(South_Africa)','wiki:False_memory'],img='Nelson Mandela',end=1994),
]
# --- part 2: gods, tricksters and heroes from six continents
E+=[
X('thor-mjolnir','Thor and Mjölnir: the thunder god whose hammer was worn against the cross',M,'myth-legend',1,800,['textual','archaeological','place names'],
 "Thor was the Norse god of thunder, strength and the protection of people and gods against the giants. His hammer Mjölnir, made by the dwarf brothers Eitri and Brokkr according to Snorri Sturluson's Prose Edda (about 1220), returned to his hand when thrown. In the Eddic poem Þrymskviða the giant Thrym steals the hammer and demands the goddess Freyja as his bride; Thor goes to the wedding disguised as Freyja and recovers it. At Ragnarök he is fated to kill the world serpent Jörmungandr, then walk nine steps and die from its venom. Small hammer pendants were commonly worn in the Viking Age, and the English word Thursday means 'Thunor's day'.",
 [('Hammer against cross','Many archaeologists suggest that hammer pendants became popular in the 10th century partly as a pagan answer to the Christian cross, as Christianity spread into Scandinavia.'),
  ('A shared thunder god?','Scholars compare Thor with other thunder gods who fight a serpent, such as the Vedic Indra who kills the serpent Vritra and the Slavic Perun, and some reconstruct a common Indo-European storm god. Others are cautious about how much can be reconstructed.')],
 ['wiki:Thor','wiki:Mjölnir','wiki:Þrymskviða'],img='Thor',dateNote='Viking Age worship; written sources 13th century'),
X('loki-bound','Loki bound: the trickster whose pain shakes the earth',M,'myth-legend',2,800,['textual'],
 "Loki is the shape-shifting trickster of Norse myth, a companion of the gods who is also their enemy. He turned himself into a mare and gave birth to Odin's eight-legged horse Sleipnir, and he fathered the wolf Fenrir and the world serpent. When he arranged the death of the beloved god Baldr, the gods bound him in a cave. A snake drips venom on to his face; his wife Sigyn catches it in a bowl, but when she leaves to empty it the venom strikes him and his writhing causes earthquakes. At Ragnarök he will break free and fight against the gods.",
 [('A god without worship','There is little evidence that Loki was ever worshipped, and scholars have long debated his origins: theories have made him a fire god, a spider-like figure, or a literary creation shaped partly by Christian ideas of the Devil.'),
  ('Bound gods elsewhere','The punishment of a bound god who will one day break loose has been compared with the Greek Prometheus and the Georgian Amirani, both chained in the Caucasus.')],
 ['wiki:Loki','wiki:Baldr','wiki:Sigyn','wiki:Gylfaginning'],img='Loki',dateNote='known mainly from 13th-century Icelandic texts'),
X('weighing-of-the-heart','The weighing of the heart: an Egyptian judgement of the dead',M,'myth-legend',1,-1475,['textual','iconographic','archaeological'],
 "The Book of the Dead is the modern name for collections of spells that helped Egyptians pass through the underworld (the Duat) into the afterlife. Its most famous chapter, Spell 125, first known from about 1475 BCE, describes the dead person's heart being weighed on scales against Maat, the goddess of truth and justice, often shown as an ostrich feather. The jackal-headed Anubis supervises the scales and the scribe god Thoth records the result. If the heart balanced, the person was declared 'true of voice' and could enter the afterlife before Osiris; if not, the monster Ammit, part crocodile, lion and hippopotamus, devoured it. The deceased also recited a list of sins they had not committed.",
 [('Morality beyond the grave','Egyptologists note that this scene is one of the few parts of the Book of the Dead with explicit moral content, and that the idea of judgement by one\'s deeds after death appears in many later religions.'),
  ('Scales of souls elsewhere','In medieval Christian art the archangel Michael is often shown weighing souls, and Zoroastrian tradition describes the dead crossing the Chinvat Bridge, which widens for the righteous and narrows for the wicked. Whether these ideas are connected or arose separately is debated.')],
 ['wiki:Book_of_the_Dead','wiki:Ammit','wiki:Maat','wiki:Papyrus_of_Ani'],img='Papyrus of Ani',dateNote='Spell 125 first known c. 1475 BCE (Hatshepsut and Thutmose III)'),
X('kalki','Kalki: the rider on the white horse who will end the dark age',M,'myth-legend',2,300,['textual'],
 "In Hindu tradition Kalki is the tenth and final avatar of the god Vishnu, who will appear at the end of the Kali Yuga, the present and darkest of the four ages of the world. The Puranas describe him riding a white horse named Devadatta and carrying a blazing sword. He will destroy evil, restore dharma (righteousness) and begin a new golden age, the Satya Yuga. Some texts say he will be born in a village called Shambhala. Kalki also appears in the Kalachakra Tantra of Tibetan Buddhism and in Sikh texts. By a traditional reckoning the Kali Yuga began in 3102 BCE and has hundreds of thousands of years still to run.",
 [('Claimed by many','Several religious founders have been identified as Kalki, including Mirza Ghulam Ahmad, founder of the Ahmadiyya movement, and Bahá\'u\'lláh in the Bahá\'í Faith.'),
  ('A world pattern','Comparative religion notes similar end-time saviour figures, such as the Buddhist Maitreya, the Islamic Mahdi and the rider on a white horse in the Christian Book of Revelation. Scholars debate whether these share roots or developed independently.')],
 ['wiki:Kalki','wiki:Kali_Yuga','wiki:Kalki_Purana'],img='Kalki',dateNote='first mentioned in the Mahabharata; Puranic accounts from the 1st millennium CE'),
X('jonah-great-fish','Jonah and the great fish, and the sailor said to have survived a whale',M,'myth-legend',2,-500,['textual','archaeological'],
 "The Book of Jonah in the Hebrew Bible tells of a prophet ordered by God to preach to Nineveh, the capital of Assyria. Jonah flees by ship in the other direction; a storm rises, the sailors throw him overboard, and a 'great fish' (Hebrew dag gadol) swallows him. After three days and nights he is spat out on land, goes to Nineveh, and the city repents and is spared. Jonah is also a prophet in Islam, and a chapter of the Quran, Yunus, is named after him. Many modern scholars consider the book a later story, partly satirical, rather than history.",
 [('James Bartley','From 1891 newspapers reported that a sailor named James Bartley had been swallowed by a sperm whale and found alive in its stomach. The historian Edward B. Davis investigated and found many inconsistencies: the ship named, the Star of the East, was not a whaling vessel. The story is generally regarded as a hoax.'),
  ('A palace under Jonah\'s tomb','The shrine of Nabi Yunus in Mosul, revered as Jonah\'s tomb, was blown up by Islamic State in 2014. When the group was driven out in 2017, tunnels it had dug beneath the site revealed the remains of an Assyrian palace with inscriptions of King Esarhaddon.')],
 ['wiki:Jonah','wiki:Book_of_Jonah','wiki:James_Bartley','wiki:Al-Nabi_Yunus_Mosque'],img='Jonah',dateNote='book usually dated to the Persian period, 5th-4th century BCE'),
X('momotaro','Momotarō, the Peach Boy: a folk hero drafted into war',M,'myth-legend',2,1700,['textual','oral tradition'],
 "In the Japanese folk tale an elderly, childless couple find a giant peach floating down a river. Inside is a baby boy, whom they name Momotarō, 'Peach Boy'. When he grows up he sets out to fight the oni (ogres) of Onigashima, the ogre island, taking millet dumplings that win him three companions: a dog, a monkey and a pheasant. They defeat the oni and return with treasure. The story became widely known in the Edo period and was a standard tale in Japanese schoolbooks.",
 [('A wartime hero','During the Second World War Momotarō was used in propaganda, with the oni standing for the Western Allies; the historian John Dower discussed this in War Without Mercy (1986). The tale disappeared from Japanese schoolbooks after the war.'),
  ('Okayama\'s claim','Okayama Prefecture promotes Momotarō as a local hero, linking him with the legend of Prince Kibitsuhiko who defeated an ogre. Scholars note that this link was developed in modern times.'),
  ('Babies on the water','A child found floating on a river is a widespread story pattern, seen also in the tales of Moses, Sargon of Akkad and Romulus and Remus.')],
 ['wiki:Momotarō','wiki:Kibitsuhiko-no-mikoto'],img='Momotarō',dateNote='written versions from the Edo period'),
X('glooscap','Glooscap: the Wabanaki giant who shaped the land',M,'myth-legend',2,1600,['oral tradition','textual'],
 "Glooscap (Klóskap and other spellings) is the culture hero of the Wabanaki peoples, including the Mi'kmaq, of Atlantic Canada and the north-eastern United States. In the stories he shapes the land and its animals, defeats monsters, and battles an evil twin, whom in one legend he turns to stone. One tale says he became a giant beaver and slapped his tail so hard that he created five islands in the Bay of Fundy, and his home is said to be Cape Blomidon in Nova Scotia. The stories were written down in the 19th century by the missionary Silas Rand and the folklorist Charles Godfrey Leland.",
 [('One of a family','Scholars compare Glooscap with other Algonquian culture heroes, such as the Ojibwe Nanabozho and the Cree Wisakedjak, who also shape the world and mix wisdom with mischief.'),
  ('Collected and reshaped','Leland\'s 1884 book Algonquin Legends compared the stories with Norse myth, and modern scholars say he sometimes reshaped them to fit his own theories.')],
 ['wiki:Glooscap','wiki:Wabanaki_Confederacy','wiki:Charles_Godfrey_Leland'],img='Glooscap',dateNote='oral tradition; recorded from the 19th century'),
X('spider-grandmother','Spider Grandmother: the weaver who led people up through the worlds',M,'myth-legend',2,1300,['oral tradition','ethnographic'],
 "Spider Grandmother (Hopi Kokyangwuti; Navajo Na'ashjé'ii Asdzáá, Spider Woman) is a helper and teacher of humans in the traditions of many peoples of the American Southwest. In Hopi tradition she guides living beings upward through a series of earlier worlds, teaching them to weave and make pottery, until they emerge into the present world. In Navajo tradition Spider Woman taught people to weave on the loom. Spider Rock, a tall sandstone spire in Canyon de Chelly, Arizona, is said to be her home.",
 [('Worlds that end','The Hopi account of earlier worlds left behind because people turned away from the creator is one of many traditions of successive world ages, alongside the Aztec Five Suns and the Hindu yugas.')],
 ['wiki:Spider_Grandmother','wiki:Canyon_de_Chelly_National_Monument'],img='Canyon de Chelly National Monument',dateNote='oral traditions; recorded from the late 19th century'),
X('pachamama','Pachamama: the Andean Earth Mother, from mountain offerings to the River Tiber',M,'myth-legend',2,1500,['ethnographic','textual','oral tradition'],
 "Pachamama, 'Mother Earth' or 'Mother World' in Quechua and Aymara, is revered by Indigenous peoples of the Andes as the source of fertility, harvests and life. Her main ritual, the challa or pago ('payment'), takes place throughout August, when people offer food, drink, coca leaves and other gifts to the earth. After the Spanish conquest, missionaries tried to suppress her worship, and in many places she became associated with the Virgin Mary. In the 21st century the constitutions of Ecuador (2008) and laws in Bolivia have recognised rights of nature using the language of Pachamama.",
 [('The statues in the river','In October 2019 wooden carvings of a pregnant woman were displayed in Rome during a Vatican synod on the Amazon. The media called them Pachamama; assailants stole some and threw them into the River Tiber, calling them idols. Pope Francis apologised, and church leaders disagreed over what the figures represented.')],
 ['wiki:Pachamama','wiki:Synod_of_Bishops_for_the_Pan-Amazon_region','wiki:Rights_of_nature'],img='Pachamama',dateNote='pre-Hispanic origins; recorded by Spanish chroniclers from the 16th century'),
X('coatlicue','Coatlicue: the Aztec goddess too frightening to display',M,'myth-legend',1,1450,['archaeological','textual','iconographic'],
 "In Aztec myth Coatlicue, 'Snakes-Her-Skirt', was sweeping on the hill of Coatepec when a ball of feathers fell and made her pregnant. Her daughter Coyolxauhqui and her 400 sons, the stars of the southern sky, set out to kill her for this dishonour. But her son Huitzilopochtli, the Mexica war and sun god, was born fully armed, killed Coyolxauhqui and threw her body down the hill, where it broke apart. A colossal Coatlicue statue with a head of two serpents and a skirt of snakes was carved for Tenochtitlan in the 15th century; it is now in the National Museum of Anthropology in Mexico City.",
 [('Buried twice','The statue was buried after the Spanish conquest and dug up in 1790. Officials feared it would be revered, or found it too disturbing, and reburied it at the university; it was uncovered in 1803 so that Alexander von Humboldt could study it, buried again, and finally recovered in 1823.'),
  ('The myth found in stone','In 1978 electricity workers in Mexico City struck a huge stone disc showing the broken body of Coyolxauhqui. It lay at the foot of the Great Temple, matching the myth, and led to the excavation of the Templo Mayor.'),
  ('Sky reading','Many scholars read the myth as the sun (Huitzilopochtli) defeating the moon (Coyolxauhqui) and the stars at each dawn; others stress its political meaning for the Mexica.')],
 ['wiki:Coatlicue','wiki:Coatlicue_statue','wiki:Coyolxāuhqui','wiki:Templo_Mayor'],img='Coatlicue statue',dateNote='statue c. 15th century; found 1790; Coyolxauhqui stone found 1978'),
X('mwindo-epic','The Mwindo epic: a Congo hero sealed in a drum',M,'myth-legend',2,1900,['oral tradition','ethnographic'],
 "The Mwindo epic is a long heroic story of the Nyanga people of the eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo, performed by bards with song, dance and music before local audiences. The chief Shemwindo ordered that only daughters be born to his wives, but Mwindo was born a son, already able to speak and carrying a magic conga scepter. His father tried to kill him with spears, then to bury him alive, and finally sealed him inside a drum and threw it into a river. Mwindo floated away to his aunt, travelled through the underworld and the sky, defeated a dragon and his father, and returned to rule wisely. A version was recorded by Daniel Biebuyck and Kahombo Mateene and published in 1969.",
 [('A universal hero?','Folklorists compare Mwindo\'s story with the hero pattern found in many cultures, including miraculous birth, an attempt to kill the child, exile, and a return in triumph. Others stress its specifically Nyanga values of humility and responsibility.')],
 ['wiki:Mwindo_epic','wiki:Nyanga_people'],img='Nyanga people',dateNote='oral tradition; recorded 1950s, published 1969'),
X('nanabozho','Nanabozho, the muskrat and Turtle Island',M,'myth-legend',2,1600,['oral tradition','textual'],
 "Nanabozho (also Nanabush, Wenabozho) is the shape-shifting trickster and culture hero of the Ojibwe and other Anishinaabe peoples. In one widespread story a great flood covers the world. Nanabozho, floating with the surviving animals, asks them to dive for earth. The beaver and the loon fail; the muskrat dives longest and comes up dead but with mud on its nose. Nanabozho places the mud on the back of a turtle, and it grows into the land, Turtle Island, a name many Indigenous people use for North America. Similar 'earth-diver' myths are known across North America and northern Asia.",
 [('The Sleeping Giant','The Sleeping Giant, a mesa on Lake Superior near Thunder Bay, Ontario, is identified in one Ojibwe legend as Nanabozho turned to stone after the secret location of a rich silver mine, now Silver Islet, was revealed to white men.'),
  ('Earth-divers across continents','Folklorists note that earth-diver stories appear among peoples from Siberia to the Americas, and some suggest they may have travelled with the first people who crossed into the Americas. This is debated.')],
 ['wiki:Nanabozho','wiki:Turtle_Island','wiki:Sleeping_Giant_(Ontario)'],img='Sleeping Giant (Ontario)',dateNote='oral tradition; recorded from the 19th century'),
]
